The Muncipality of Milan put in place several action to reduce emissions related to food consumption, such as:
- shifting of the school menu towards more sustainable and plant-based option. This action is also measured through the Cool Food Pledge Initiative which highlighted a reduction of 34% of GHG emission related to Milan school meals programme since 2015 by increasing legumes, tubers, vegetables and reducing meet (in particular the red one);
- since 2016 school canteens in Milan are plastic free: they serve tap water and all table wear is compostable or reusable;
- through public food procurement Milano Ristorazione exploits local production (such as for the rice, produced within the boarder of the City) and short supply chain. In compliance with CAM regulation, Milano Ristorazione sources the highest rate possible of organic and fairtrade product;
- the logistic of meals distribution is optimised in order to reduce emissions related to transport and the delivery is carried out by using electric van. The possibility to introduce also cargo-bike for short distance delivery is currently under testing:
- several educational activities are organised both for kids, families, educators, teachers, cookers and Milano Ristorazione's kitchen staff in order to promote healthier and more sustainable food habits;
- a service of collection and redistribution of bread and fruit not consumed during the meals in school canteens is active in some school of the city through a collaboration with a local NGO. In addiction, for all the school that wish to do, a reusable doggy bag is available with the scope of not wasting bread, fruit and dessert left at the end of the lunch;
- since 2019 is active in the City, and it is still growing in participation, efficiency and capacity, the Food Waste Hub network. Nowadays it can count on 7 Hubs located around the city (including one within the Milan's Wholesale Market) which makes possible to collect and redistribute food surpluses from supermarket chains, big retailers and working canteens. The possibility to connect the network with local street market and local school canteens is under study.

The Land Use Plan listed in 8.2 (row 3), sets obligations and recommendations regarding construction and demolition within the Parisian territory, that must be respected for any project to be approved by the City. 

Demolition wise, the general ruling states that for existing buildings, conservation, transformation or extension must always be privileged instead of demolition and rebuilding (UG 2.3). 

Regarding construction, in order to be approved, they must be under an carbon footprint index threshold (580 kgeqCO2/m² for residential and 710 kgeqCO2/m² for offices). Additionally, all construction must use recycled, renewable, bio-sourced, geo-sourced or reused material; contributing to the overall decrease of the construction's footprint, on top of targets of increased thermal comfort.

Prefacing the PLU, the City also developed with its partner a guideline document for priorities for sustainable constructions. The Parisian Construction Pact defines a new way of building the Paris of tomorrow: a city with high environmental standards. This document, which outlines 10 objectives, was a first step before the revision of the Bioclimatic Land Use Plan.

The 10 objectives of the Pact:
1. Promote diversity in projects;
2. Avoid soil sealing and enhance biodiversity;
3. Protect and develop green spaces, creating cool islands;
4. Preserve heritage, integrating projects into their immediate environment;
5. Prioritize restructuring over demolition, reuse over waste;
6. Energy sobriety and low carbon emissions;
7. Favor optimal, high-quality construction sites with low environmental impact, fostering economic opportunities;
8. Emphasize the collective aspect of the project: transition urbanism and citizen participation;
9. Create 'commons,' promote mixed uses, mutualization, and democratization of buildings;
10. Prioritize reversibility, adaptability, and modularity of the projects.The Paris Land and Use plan which includes a section on energy and environmental performance of buildings (Article UG.15 in the document) (https://cdn.paris.fr/paris/2020/02/26/16107d9c38a049046444a7b6301df1aa.ai)

This section focuses on logistics, for other transport related plans, see section 8.2. Additionally, the City of Paris, through its 2024-2030 Climate Action Plan, advocates for the reduction of plane travel towards Paris and for the increase of train options and affordability.

In September 2013, the city published a Charter for Sustainable Urban Logistics. This defined a new logistics organisation dealing not only with deliveries to businesses or private individuals, but also the removal of household, industrial, building and office waste. Following this first document, the City actualized its charter and adopted the Urban Logistics Strategy (2022-2026). 

The City of Paris's urban logistics strategy, adopted by the Paris Council in 2022, is a founding document that aims to transform the management of freight flows in the heart of the capital. This approach addresses the major challenges of Parisian urban logistics through six strategic axes:
- Land and logistics real estate;
- Sharing public space;
- Working conditions for delivery drivers;
- The availability of local businesses and producers;
- Construction site logistics;
- The ecological transition of freight transport.

Its objective is to create a more efficient, sustainable, and inclusive logistics ecosystem by gradually undertaking concrete actions along these axes, in a coherent and coordinated manner, with a broad network of economic stakeholders and professional federations.

